e. Choose $d < r < \\min(D, D_*)$ with $\\rho$ the only pole in $\\overline B(z, r)$. As $\\overline B(\\sigma, r)$ has no pole, Step 2 gives\n\\[ |A_k(z)| + \\Re A_k(\\sigma) \\ge |c|\\, d^{-k-1} - O(r^{-k}), \\]\nwhich exceeds $C D_*^{-k}$ for large $k \\in K$, contradicting $(*)$. We cannot argue at $z = \\sigma$ itself: there the conjugate poles $\\rho_1, \\bar\\rho_1$ are equally close and their contributions to $A_k(\\sigma)$ may cancel.\n\n(b) \\emph{Every pole has $\\sigma - \\Re\\rho \\ge \\min(D, D_*)$.} Applied to $\\rho_0$ this gives $D < D_*$. A nearest pole $\\rho$ to $\\sigma$ satisfies $D = |\\sigma - \\rho| \\ge \\sigma - \\Re\\rho \\ge D$, so $\\rho = \\beta = \\sigma - D$ is real and the only pole at distance $D$. Its residue $c_\\beta$ is positive by \\hyperref[8JS7]{the corollary}. By Step 2 with $D < r < D_*$, and $(*)$ at $\\tau = 0$,\n\\[ 2 c_\\beta D^{-k-1} - O(r^{-k}) \\le |A_k(\\sigma)| + \\Re A_k(\\sigma) \\le C\\, D_*^{-k}, \\]\nwhich is false for large $k \\in K$. Here the sign of $c_\\beta$ is essential: with $c_\\beta < 0$ the left side would be small and the one-sided bound $(*)$ would give no information.","status":"ai_generated","generated_by":"claude","generator_detail":"claude-opus-5-5","reviewed_by":null,"review_notes":null,"position":1}', 'claude');
